1562:- FINDING: this is the THIRD occurrence of the identical class inside this one artifact, and the first two are recorded here by the same author. Entry 1015: the "nineteen §15.2 rows" claim against an eighteen-row table. Entry 1157: two of four published fan-in rows wrong, root cause named at the time as "prose nobody measured". The response to the second was `TestREADMEFanInTableIsDerivedFromTheMeasuredProjection`, which parses the README and re-derives every cell — and it sits two hundred lines from a sentence that restates instead of derives and is off by one. The tool for the class existed, in the repository, written for this exact failure, and was never pointed at the neighbouring paragraph.
1563-- DECISION: the general rule, worth stating once. A figure published beside a measurement must be an INPUT to the test, not a second copy of the answer. Three instances in one artifact is not carelessness; it is the natural failure mode of prose sitting near data. The only fix that holds is parsing the prose and re-deriving every number in it from the thing it describes.
